% ============================================================================
% heart-chamber-vessel — the named vessel(s)/valve(s) each heart chamber
% connects to, decoded from spans already sitting unused inside
% `heart-chambers.adj`'s own already-quoted NIH/NLM StatPearls sentences
% (adj-facts-stdlib, ANATOMY).
% ============================================================================
% A sibling to the already-shipped `heart-chambers.adj`
% (`heart_chamber_function(chamber, function)`, right_atrium/right_ventricle/
% left_atrium/left_ventricle → receives_blood_from_body/pumps_blood_to_lungs/
% receives_blood_from_lungs/pumps_blood_to_body): that table's own header
% quotes five StatPearls sentences (the left atrium's span is two) that name
% each chamber's FUNCTION (what it does to blood) AND the NAMED vessel(s)/
% valve(s) that blood passes through to get there -- a fact the
% chamber/function schema had no room for. (This paragraph used to say
% "four StatPearls sentences", each naming both "in the same breath"; the
% left-atrium sentence alone says only "these veins", as noted below.)
%
%   right_atrium → superior_vena_cava, inferior_vena_cava
%     "...via the superior and inferior vena cavae."
%
%   right_ventricle → pulmonic_valve, pulmonary_artery
%     "...across the pulmonic valve, and into the pulmonary artery..."
%
%   left_atrium → pulmonary_veins
%     "...collected by the four pulmonary veins..."
%
%   left_ventricle → aortic_valve
%     "...through the aortic valve."
%
% New `heart_chamber_vessel(chamber, vessel)` table decodes those named
% structures as their own rows -- six in total (right_atrium and
% right_ventricle each name TWO structures; left_atrium and left_ventricle
% each name ONE). This is a genuinely different axis from the already-shipped
% `valve_separates(valve, boundary)` table in `heart-valves.adj`: that table
% is keyed by VALVE and names the two CHAMBERS it sits between; this table is
% keyed by CHAMBER and names the VESSEL(S)/VALVE(S) attached to it -- neither
% schema absorbs the other (`valve_separates` never mentions the vena cavae or
% the pulmonary veins at all, since those are vessels, not valves). Recall is
% a binding query:
%
%     ? heart_chamber_vessel(left_ventricle, $Vessel)  % aortic_valve
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5, a TWO-column table like the
% parent's own): each `row` lowers to a ground relation
% `heart_chamber_vessel(chamber, vessel)` carrying the citation, so the
% lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query -- the engine returns the
% vessel WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model calls, and abstains on
% anything that is not one of the four chambers (it never invents a vessel).
% The query also runs BACKWARD -- bind a vessel and recall the chamber it
% connects to:
%
%     ? heart_chamber_vessel($C, aortic_valve)  % left_ventricle
%
% A recall on `septum` -- a real cardiac structure, but not one of the four
% chambers -- correctly abstains rather than inventing a connected vessel for
% it (the same honest-abstention example `heart-chambers.adj`'s own header
% already uses).
%
% Terminology note: `heart-chambers.adj`'s StatPearls source calls the
% right-ventricle-to-lungs valve the "pulmonic valve" and its downstream
% vessel the "pulmonary artery" -- different wording from `heart-valves.adj`'s
% NCI SEER source, which calls the SAME valve "pulmonary" and its downstream
% vessel the "pulmonary trunk". Both are preserved verbatim per-source rather
% than homogenized, exactly as `heart-valves.adj`'s own header already does
% for its own "pulmonary trunk" (not "pulmonary artery") wording.
%
% Provenance: the SAME NIH/NLM StatPearls "Anatomy, Thorax, Heart" page that
% `heart-chambers.adj` cites. This paragraph used to say its spans were
% reproduced "byte-for-byte" with "no new WebFetch"; they are now measured
% against the page itself (below). `trust authoritative` -- the same tier
% the parent table
% already carries (StatPearls is a primary U.S. government NIH/NLM
% reference).
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts, feedback_no_byte_arithmetic_for_llm.)
%
% EACH ROW CARRIES ITS OWN SPAN (ADJ-TABLES RS-5e, #14986). The envelope used
% to be the right-atrium sentence, the primary source of all six answers,
% with the other three chambers' sentences as table-level `cites`. Now:
%
%   - both right-atrium rows take the right-atrium sentence, which names
%     both vena cavae;
%   - both right-ventricle rows take the right-ventricle sentence, which
%     names the pulmonic valve and the pulmonary artery;
%   - the left-atrium row takes two contiguous sentences, "This oxygenated
%     blood is collected by the four pulmonary veins, two from each lung.
%     All four of these veins open into the left atrium ...". The
%     left-atrium sentence alone says only "these veins", so it never named
%     the pulmonary veins; the sentence before it does;
%   - the left-ventricle row takes the left-ventricle sentence.
%
% The envelope is now a framing sentence from the page, which names no
% chamber and no vessel:
%
%     "The heart is a muscular organ situated in the center of the chest
%      behind the sternum."
%
% Measured 2026-09-15 on the raw page (HTTP 200; a nonsense path on the same
% host returns 404), with inline tags removed without a space and only ASCII
% whitespace collapsed: the four row spans and the envelope each occur
% exactly once, each inside a `<p>`, and the four ellipsis fragments quoted
% above each occur once. Every row's page is the envelope's, so no row
% restates a `locator` line or `trust` (ADJ-TABLES §4).
% ============================================================================

table heart_chamber_vessel {
    columns chamber, vessel

    row (right_atrium, superior_vena_cava) {
        source "The right atrium receives deoxygenated blood from the entire body except for the lungs (the systemic circulation) via the superior and inferior vena cavae."
    }
    row (right_atrium, inferior_vena_cava) {
        source "The right atrium receives deoxygenated blood from the entire body except for the lungs (the systemic circulation) via the superior and inferior vena cavae."
    }
    row (right_ventricle, pulmonic_valve) {
        source "The right ventricle pumps blood through the right ventricular outflow tract, across the pulmonic valve, and into the pulmonary artery that distributes it to the lungs for oxygenation."
    }
    row (right_ventricle, pulmonary_artery) {
        source "The right ventricle pumps blood through the right ventricular outflow tract, across the pulmonic valve, and into the pulmonary artery that distributes it to the lungs for oxygenation."
    }
    row (left_atrium, pulmonary_veins) {
        source "This oxygenated blood is collected by the four pulmonary veins, two from each lung. All four of these veins open into the left atrium that acts as a collection chamber for oxygenated blood."
    }
    row (left_ventricle, aortic_valve) {
        source "The left ventricle is the main pumping chamber of the left heart, then pumps, sending freshly oxygenated blood to the systemic circulation through the aortic valve."
    }

    % THE ENVELOPE WAS THE RIGHT-ATRIUM ROW'S OWN SENTENCE, the primary source
    % of all six answers, and the other chambers' vessels were read across
    % three table-level `cites`. Each row now carries its own span, and the
    % envelope is a framing sentence that names no chamber and no vessel.
    % Every row overrides it, so it reaches no answer.
    source "The heart is a muscular organ situated in the center of the chest behind the sternum."
    locator "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK470256/"
    trust authoritative
}
